"""Owner-facing treatment and testing guidance for cancer pages.

These blocks explain what treatment categories and tests mean to an owner. They avoid
turning the page into a protocol sheet or a list of every possible oncology test.
Canine HS remains the benchmark and can keep its existing treatment/tests copy.
"""

DOG_TREATMENT = {
'lymphoma': 'Because lymphoma is usually a systemic cancer, chemotherapy is the main treatment for most dogs. A CHOP-based protocol is a common first choice for high-grade multicentric lymphoma. Other drug combinations, single agents, radiation for selected localized forms, and clinical trials can be considered depending on subtype, prior treatment and relapse status.',
'mast cell tumor': 'Surgery is the main treatment for a removable localized mast cell tumor. Radiation can help when cancer remains at the surgical site and another operation is not practical. Higher-risk, recurrent, unresectable or metastatic tumors may also need systemic treatment such as chemotherapy or a targeted tyrosine-kinase inhibitor.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Wide surgery is the main treatment when the tumor can be removed with an appropriate margin. If margins are incomplete or another wide surgery is not practical, radiation can improve local control. Chemotherapy is usually reserved for higher-grade, metastatic or otherwise high-risk tumors rather than used automatically for every soft tissue sarcoma.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'When a visceral tumor can be removed safely, surgery treats the primary bleeding mass and provides tissue for diagnosis. Because microscopic spread is a major concern, systemic chemotherapy is often discussed after surgery. Skin hemangiosarcoma may need only local treatment when it is superficial and completely removed.',
'osteosarcoma': 'Treatment has two goals: control the painful primary bone tumor and delay metastatic spread. Amputation is the most common local treatment for a limb tumor, with limb-sparing surgery or radiation used in selected cases. Chemotherapy is commonly added after local control because microscopic metastasis is frequent even when scans look clear.',
'oral melanoma': 'Local control usually means surgery when a functional complete removal is possible or radiation when surgery cannot achieve good control. Because oral melanoma can spread early, systemic or immune-based treatment may also be discussed. Clinical trials are especially relevant when the tumor is measurable or when standard options are limited.',
'melanoma': 'Treatment depends mainly on where the melanoma began. A localized skin melanoma may be treated with surgery alone, while oral, digital or mucosal melanoma may need staging, more aggressive local treatment and discussion of systemic or immune-based options. The site matters more than the word melanoma by itself.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Surgery gives the best chance of durable control when the tumor can be completely removed without unacceptable loss of function. Radiation can be used when surgery is incomplete, not feasible or intended only for palliation. Systemic treatment has a more limited role and is chosen according to site, stage and tumor behavior.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'Treatment is primarily local and depends on site. Surgery, radiation and other local approaches can all be appropriate. Small superficial skin lesions may have several curative options, while deeper or anatomically difficult tumors may need combined local treatment. Systemic treatment is reserved for selected advanced cases.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'Most dogs are treated medically because bladder and urethral location often makes complete surgery impossible. NSAID-based therapy, chemotherapy and combinations of the two are commonly used. Radiation, surgery in selected locations, stents or other interventional procedures may be added when local disease or urinary obstruction becomes the main problem.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'A solitary resectable hepatocellular carcinoma is usually treated with liver lobectomy, which can provide excellent local control. Multifocal, diffuse, invasive or metastatic disease requires a different plan and may lead to discussion of systemic treatment, interventional techniques or clinical trials.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Surgery is the main treatment for localized mammary carcinoma. The amount of tissue removed depends on how many glands are involved and their location. Chemotherapy is considered selectively for higher-risk disease, especially when pathology shows aggressive features, lymph-node involvement or metastasis.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Surgery is preferred for a mobile, resectable thyroid tumor. Radiation is useful for invasive, fixed, incompletely removed or non-surgical tumors. Radioactive iodine can be appropriate for selected iodine-avid cancers, while systemic or targeted treatment may be considered for progressive or metastatic disease.',
'prostate cancer': 'Treatment is usually multimodal because complete surgical removal is difficult in many dogs. NSAIDs, chemotherapy and radiation can all be part of the plan. Procedures to keep urine flowing may be necessary when the tumor narrows the urethra, and local treatment is chosen according to anatomy and metastatic stage.',
'primary lung tumor': 'For a solitary resectable primary lung tumor, surgical removal of the affected lung lobe is the usual treatment. Additional chemotherapy or another systemic treatment may be considered when pathology is high risk, lymph nodes are involved, the tumor cannot be removed completely or metastatic disease is present.',
'glioma': 'Radiation is a common tumor-directed treatment for canine glioma. Surgery or biopsy is possible in selected locations, and some centers offer investigational local-delivery or immune-based treatments. Anti-seizure drugs and steroids can control symptoms but do not replace treatment of the tumor itself.',
'meningioma': 'Surgery, radiation or a combination can provide meaningful local control. Surgery is best suited to tumors that are safely accessible, while radiation is often chosen when surgery is not feasible, incomplete or not preferred. Medications help control seizures and inflammation but do not remove or shrink the tumor by themselves.',
'nasal tumor': 'Radiation is the main definitive treatment for most canine nasal tumors because complete surgery is rarely practical. Chemotherapy or targeted drugs may be added for selected histologies or advanced disease. Palliative radiation can be used when the goal is symptom relief rather than long-term local control.',
'leukemia': 'Treatment is systemic. Acute leukemias usually require multi-drug chemotherapy and substantial supportive care, while some chronic leukemias can be managed with oral chemotherapy or targeted drugs. The exact plan depends on whether the leukemia is lymphoid or myeloid and acute or chronic.',
'multiple myeloma': 'Systemic drug treatment is the main therapy because myeloma affects the body rather than one isolated site. Alkylating chemotherapy with a corticosteroid is a common starting approach, with other drugs used when needed. Radiation can help painful focal bone lesions, while supportive care treats kidney injury, high calcium, anemia or hyperviscosity.',
'chemodectoma': 'Treatment depends on whether the heart-base tumor is growing or causing problems. Radiation and targeted systemic therapy can provide control in selected dogs. Surgery is difficult for many tumors because of their location, and procedures may be needed to manage fluid around the heart or other complications.',
}

CAT_TREATMENT = {
'lymphoma': 'Treatment depends on the form and grade of lymphoma. Some low-grade intestinal lymphomas can be controlled with oral chemotherapy and a corticosteroid, while high-grade disease usually needs more intensive multi-agent chemotherapy. Radiation is especially useful for selected localized forms such as nasal lymphoma.',
'mast cell tumor': 'A solitary removable skin mast cell tumor is usually treated with surgery. Splenectomy can provide useful control for splenic mast cell disease in appropriate cats. Intestinal, disseminated, recurrent or unresectable disease may need systemic treatment, but feline evidence is more limited and should not simply copy canine protocols.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Wide planned surgery is the main treatment when complete removal is feasible, especially for injection-site sarcoma. Radiation is often considered when margins are incomplete or when another wide surgery is difficult. Systemic treatment is used selectively for higher-risk or metastatic disease.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'Complete surgery is the main local treatment for resectable disease. Superficial skin tumors can behave very differently from visceral hemangiosarcoma. For internal or disseminated disease, systemic treatment may be discussed, but feline evidence is limited and the plan should be individualized.',
'osteosarcoma': 'For a resectable limb tumor, amputation is the main treatment and usually provides both pain relief and local control. Radiation can be used when surgery is not possible. Additional chemotherapy may be discussed in selected cats, particularly when metastatic risk appears higher.',
'oral melanoma': 'When complete removal is anatomically possible, surgery is the main local treatment. Radiation can be used when surgery cannot achieve useful control or for palliation. There is no well-established feline systemic standard, so systemic and immune-based approaches are usually individualized or investigational.',
'melanoma': 'Treatment depends heavily on the site. Surgery is the main local treatment for many resectable melanomas. Ocular tumors may require ophthalmic surgery, while oral or metastatic disease may need radiation or individualized systemic treatment. Canine melanoma vaccine outcomes should not be presented as feline evidence.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Surgery offers the best chance of durable control only when complete removal is realistically possible. Radiation and other local treatments can be used when surgery is not feasible or for palliation. Pain control and nutritional support are often part of treatment from the beginning.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'For cutaneous SCC, surgery, radiation, cryotherapy and electrochemotherapy can all be effective in selected cases. The best option depends on site, size and depth. Oral SCC follows a different and more difficult treatment pathway and should not be grouped with superficial skin disease.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'There is no single established feline standard. Surgery may be possible for favorably located tumors, while medical therapy, radiation and procedures to maintain urine flow can be considered according to anatomy and stage. Drug choices need to be based on feline safety rather than borrowed automatically from dogs.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'A solitary resectable hepatocellular carcinoma is usually treated with liver lobectomy and can have prolonged control. Multifocal, diffuse, invasive or metastatic liver cancer requires an individualized plan rather than the same surgical approach.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Surgery is the main treatment for localized feline mammary carcinoma, and the surgical plan is often more extensive than simply removing one visible nodule. Chemotherapy may be discussed for higher-risk, node-positive or metastatic disease, although expected benefit varies between cats.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Surgery can be appropriate for a localized removable thyroid carcinoma. Radioactive iodine may be useful for selected iodine-avid tumors, while radiation or another local or systemic treatment can be considered for invasive, recurrent or metastatic disease.',
'prostate cancer': 'There is no established feline standard because this cancer is exceptionally rare. Treatment is individualized and may include surgery in selected anatomy, radiation or systemic treatment, procedures to maintain urine flow and supportive care.',
'primary lung tumor': 'Lung lobectomy is the main treatment for a solitary resectable primary lung tumor when staging does not show prohibitive spread. Additional systemic treatment is considered selectively according to pathology, lymph-node involvement and metastatic stage.',
'glioma': 'Radiation is a common local treatment when feasible. Surgery or biopsy may be possible for selected lesions. Anti-seizure medication and steroids can control symptoms but do not treat the tumor itself. Because feline data are sparse, treatment choices often rely on specialist judgment rather than a large evidence base.',
'meningioma': 'Surgical removal is often a strong option for accessible feline meningiomas and can provide long periods of control. Radiation is useful when surgery is not feasible, incomplete or declined, or when the tumor recurs. Medications help control neurologic symptoms but are not tumor-directed treatment.',
'nasal tumor': 'Treatment depends on the biopsy result. Radiation is important for many feline nasal carcinomas and other non-lymphoid tumors, while nasal lymphoma follows a lymphoma treatment pathway and may use radiation, chemotherapy or both.',
'leukemia': 'Acute leukemias generally require systemic chemotherapy and substantial supportive care, while some chronic leukemias can be managed less intensively with oral therapy. The exact plan depends on cell lineage, blood counts, clinical stability and whether FeLV is relevant to the case.',
'multiple myeloma': 'Systemic therapy is the main treatment because feline myeloma is generally disseminated. Alkylating chemotherapy with a corticosteroid is commonly considered. Radiation can help painful focal bone lesions, while supportive care addresses kidney injury, high calcium, anemia or other complications.',
'chemodectoma': 'Treatment is individualized because feline chemodectoma is extremely rare. Radiation, surgery in selected anatomy, medical treatment and procedures to control fluid may all be considered. There is no dependable feline standard that should be inferred from canine studies.',
'histiocytic sarcoma': 'For localized resectable disease, surgery may provide local control and radiation can be considered when complete surgery is not feasible. There is no well-established feline systemic standard for disseminated histiocytic sarcoma, so treatment is individualized and research options may be worth discussing.',
}

DOG_TESTS = {
'lymphoma': 'Useful tests are the ones that identify the lymphoma type and establish a safe baseline for treatment. Flow cytometry, PARR or immunohistochemistry may help distinguish B-cell from T-cell disease or clarify an uncertain diagnosis. CBC and chemistry are commonly obtained before treatment, while additional staging is chosen according to the form of lymphoma and whether the result would change management.',
'mast cell tumor': 'The pathology report should include grade, mitotic activity and margins. Sampling the draining lymph node can be important even when it feels normal. KIT or c-kit testing is most useful when it may influence prognosis, targeted-drug selection or trial eligibility rather than as a routine add-on for every tumor.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'The most important information usually comes from pathology: exact diagnosis, grade and measured surgical margins. Imaging is selected according to tumor size, location and grade, especially when it will change surgery or staging. Keep pathology blocks and slides available if review, molecular testing or a trial may be considered.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'CBC and chemistry help assess blood loss and organ function. Chest and abdominal imaging are commonly used for staging, and echocardiography is useful when a heart-base or right-atrial lesion is suspected. Additional tumor assays should have a clear purpose before they are ordered.',
'osteosarcoma': 'Imaging of the affected bone confirms the extent of local damage, while chest imaging looks for visible lung metastasis. CT can be useful when it would change surgical or radiation planning. Blood work, including alkaline phosphatase, can add prognostic context but does not replace staging.',
'oral melanoma': 'Pathology confirms the diagnosis and should include features relevant to biologic behavior. Regional lymph nodes may need sampling even when they are not enlarged, and chest imaging is commonly used to look for spread. Local imaging is useful when bone or deep soft-tissue invasion affects surgery or radiation planning.',
'melanoma': 'Testing should match the site. Pathology is central, and poorly pigmented tumors may need additional immunohistochemistry. For higher-risk sites such as oral or digital melanoma, lymph-node assessment and chest staging can be useful. Molecular testing is best reserved for a specific treatment or trial question.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Biopsy confirms the tumor type, and local imaging helps show how much jaw bone or nearby soft tissue is involved before major surgery or radiation. Regional lymph-node sampling and chest staging are considered when they would change treatment.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'Biopsy and site-appropriate imaging are more useful than a generic cancer panel. Imaging is most helpful when the tumor is large, deep, near bone or in an anatomically difficult location. Regional lymph-node assessment is considered for higher-risk sites.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'Urinalysis, urine culture and imaging of the bladder, urethra and kidneys help define the problem and rule out infection or obstruction. A urine-based BRAF test can support the diagnosis in some dogs, but tissue or cytology may still be needed depending on the case and trial requirements.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'Abdominal ultrasound or CT helps determine whether the tumor is solitary and surgically removable and shows its relationship to major vessels. Blood chemistry helps assess liver function and surgical risk. Cytology can be inconclusive, so biopsy or surgical pathology may be needed for a definitive diagnosis.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Chest imaging and regional lymph-node assessment are commonly used for staging. Pathology after surgery should report tumor type, grade, margins and lymphovascular invasion when possible. Additional biomarker testing is most useful when it has a clear treatment or trial purpose.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Neck imaging helps show whether the mass is mobile, invasive or involving major vessels. Thyroid hormone testing and chest staging may be useful, while scintigraphy or iodine-uptake assessment matters mainly when radioactive iodine is being considered.',
'prostate cancer': 'Imaging should define the prostate, urethra, bladder and regional lymph nodes and look for metastatic disease when appropriate. Kidney values matter when urine flow is compromised. If tissue confirmation is needed, the safest sampling method should be chosen based on anatomy and whether the result will change treatment.',
'primary lung tumor': 'Chest CT is often the most useful test when surgery is being considered because it shows the primary mass, chest lymph nodes and additional nodules better than radiographs. Pathology after removal confirms tumor type and grade, and lymph-node sampling can add important staging information.',
'glioma': 'MRI is the central test for diagnosis and treatment planning. The key question is whether the imaging appearance is sufficient for the planned treatment or whether biopsy would change management enough to justify its risk. Trial enrollment may require histologic confirmation.',
'meningioma': 'MRI is the main planning test because it defines location, size and relationship to nearby brain structures. Tissue diagnosis and grade are obtained if surgery or biopsy is performed. Additional testing is useful only when it changes surgical, radiation or trial planning.',
'nasal tumor': 'CT of the nasal cavity plus biopsy are the core tests. CT shows local extent, including orbit or cribriform-plate involvement, while biopsy identifies the actual tumor type. Regional lymph-node sampling is added when it could change staging or treatment.',
'leukemia': 'CBC with blood-smear review is the starting point. Flow cytometry, immunophenotyping, PARR and bone-marrow sampling may be needed to distinguish acute from chronic disease and lymphoid from myeloid leukemia. These tests matter because the treatment plan depends on that classification.',
'multiple myeloma': 'CBC and chemistry, calcium, serum and urine protein studies, imaging for bone lesions and bone-marrow evaluation may all contribute. The important goal is to confirm that this is systemic plasma-cell disease and identify kidney, bone, blood or protein-related complications that need treatment.',
'chemodectoma': 'Echocardiography and cross-sectional imaging help define the mass, fluid accumulation, compression of nearby structures and change over time. Tissue confirmation is not always safe or necessary, so sampling should be pursued only when the result would meaningfully change management.',
}

CAT_TESTS = {
'lymphoma': 'The most useful tests establish the anatomic form and grade. Cytology or biopsy may be enough in some cases, while flow cytometry, PARR or immunohistochemistry can help when the diagnosis is uncertain. CBC, chemistry and site-appropriate staging are commonly useful before treatment.',
'mast cell tumor': 'For a solitary skin tumor, pathology after removal may be all that is needed. With multiple, recurrent or visceral disease, CBC, abdominal imaging and sampling of suspicious spleen, intestine or lymph nodes can help define extent. Canine mast-cell grading systems should not be applied to cats.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Biopsy and cross-sectional imaging are especially useful before radical surgery for a large, fixed or suspected injection-site sarcoma. Pathology should report grade and measured margins. Keep blocks and slides available for review or trial enrollment.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'CBC and chemistry help identify anemia and organ effects. Abdominal imaging and chest staging are useful for visceral disease, but imaging may not show every microscopic or multifocal lesion. Pathology, and sometimes immunohistochemistry, confirms the diagnosis.',
'osteosarcoma': 'Imaging of the affected bone, chest staging and pathology are the core tests. CT may be useful for difficult surgical sites or when better lung staging would change the plan. Additional drug-response or molecular testing is not routinely useful unless tied to a specific treatment or trial.',
'oral melanoma': 'Histopathology with an appropriate immunohistochemistry panel can be important because poorly pigmented feline oral tumors can be misclassified. Imaging defines local bone and soft-tissue invasion, while regional lymph nodes and the chest are assessed when metastatic staging will change treatment.',
'melanoma': 'Histopathology is central, especially for poorly pigmented tumors. Testing should be chosen according to the primary site; ocular, oral and cutaneous melanomas do not share one staging system. Regional nodes and chest or abdominal staging are considered when the result would change management.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Biopsy confirms the diagnosis, and CT is often useful for defining jaw, tongue and other local invasion before surgery or radiation. Regional lymph nodes may be sampled when staging would change treatment. Additional assays should have a clear practical purpose.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'Biopsy is useful when the diagnosis is uncertain or treatment will be substantial. Imaging is most helpful for large, deep or invasive lesions, especially when bone involvement is possible. Early superficial skin lesions often need less extensive staging than oral disease.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'Ultrasound or CT helps define tumor location, obstruction and kidney involvement. Urinalysis and culture can identify concurrent infection. A tissue or cytologic diagnosis should be obtained using a method appropriate for urinary tract tumors, and staging is added when it will change management.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'Abdominal ultrasound or CT helps define which liver lobes are involved, the relationship to major vessels and whether surgery is realistic. Chest staging and assessment for abdominal spread are reasonable before major surgery. Tissue diagnosis matters because different liver cancers behave differently.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Chest imaging and regional lymph-node assessment help stage the disease. Pathology after surgery should report grade, margins and lymphovascular invasion when possible. Additional biomarker testing should be ordered only when it may change treatment or trial eligibility.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Thyroid hormone testing alone cannot prove carcinoma. Neck imaging and chest staging help assess invasion and spread, while scintigraphy can be useful when radioactive iodine is being considered. Tissue diagnosis is pursued when it will change the treatment plan.',
'prostate cancer': 'Ultrasound or CT helps define the prostate, urethra, bladder and regional lymph nodes. Chest imaging is reasonable because metastasis can occur. Cytology or histopathology is used to confirm tumor type when a sample can be obtained safely and when the result will alter management.',
'primary lung tumor': 'Chest CT is useful for defining the primary mass, intrathoracic lymph nodes and pleural disease before surgery. Suspicious lymph nodes should be sampled when practical, and pathology after lobectomy provides the tumor type and grade that guide any discussion of additional treatment.',
'glioma': 'MRI is the central imaging test. Ask how confident the imaging diagnosis is and whether biopsy would change treatment enough to justify its risk. Cerebrospinal fluid sampling is not automatically appropriate for a brain mass and should be considered only when it is safe and useful.',
'meningioma': 'MRI is the key planning test because it shows whether the mass is accessible and how it affects nearby brain tissue. Histopathology provides confirmation and grade if surgery is performed. Additional tests should be selected only when they change the treatment plan.',
'nasal tumor': 'CT of the nasal cavity plus biopsy are usually the core tests. The biopsy result distinguishes lymphoma from carcinoma and other tumor types, while CT shows local extent and involvement of the orbit, cribriform plate or regional structures.',
'leukemia': 'CBC with blood-smear review, bone-marrow evaluation and immunophenotyping or other lineage testing may be needed to classify the leukemia. FeLV and FIV testing are relevant but answer a different question; they do not replace classification of the leukemia itself.',
'multiple myeloma': 'CBC, chemistry, calcium, serum and urine protein studies, imaging for bone lesions and bone-marrow evaluation may all contribute. The goal is to confirm plasma-cell disease and identify anemia, kidney injury, high calcium, bone damage and abnormal proteins that may need immediate treatment.',
'chemodectoma': 'Echocardiography and chest CT can define the mass, fluid, vascular invasion and compression of nearby structures. Tissue diagnosis can be difficult or risky at the heart base, so sampling is most useful when it is safe and likely to change management.',
'histiocytic sarcoma': 'Pathology with appropriate immunohistochemistry is important to confirm histiocytic origin. Imaging and site-specific sampling help determine whether disease is localized or present in several organs. Keep pathology blocks and slides available because expert review or research enrollment may require them.',
}
